  "image_name": "3acd5349024695886d5f663a3bcf5ef4.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure compares a polycrystalline material in a pristine state with the same material after electrochemical cycling, showing how different grains develop distinct defects and structural changes. At left, a four-grain particle is shown first as clean and compact, then after cycling surrounded by a CEI layer and containing surface voids or damage features.\n\nThe crystal models on the right illustrate grain-specific behavior. In the pristine state, the grains appear relatively ordered, with one grain containing a twin and another showing a regular layered or honeycomb-like arrangement. After cycling, several disorder mechanisms emerge. One grain shows local densification near the surface, with added metal defects in octahedral and tetrahedral sites. Another develops stacking faults associated with changes in Li/M ordering. A third grain contains an antiphase boundary, indicating a shift between neighboring ordered domains. The final grain shows a visibly distorted framework in the projected atomic network.\n\nOverall, the figure conveys that cycling transforms an initially well-ordered, multi-grain particle into a chemically and structurally heterogeneous one, where each grain responds differently through densification, faulting, boundary formation, and lattice distortion.",
